{"name":"Te Papa Green","hex":"#2b4b40","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"papa","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Papa of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Dad, daddy, father; a familiar or old-fashioned term of address to one’s father.","A pet name for one's grandfather."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/papa"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Te Papa","blocks":[{"text":"The Museum of New Zealand Te Papa Tongarewa is New Zealand's national museum and is located in Wellington. Usually known as Te Papa, it opened in 1998 after the merging of the National Museum of New Zealand and the National Art Gallery. An average of more than 1.1 million people visit every year, making it the 58th-most-visited art gallery in the world in 2023. Te Papa operates under a bicultural philosophy and emphasises the living stories behind its cultural treasures. Te Papa charges an entrance fee of $35 for international visitors but has free entry for all New Zealanders. Some temporary exhibitions are ticketed, but may have occasional free days."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Museum_of_New_Zealand_Te_Papa_Tongarewa_unsaturated.jpg/330px-Museum_of_New_Zealand_Te_Papa_Tongarewa_unsaturated.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Te_Papa"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/James_Hector_1900.jpg/500px-James_Hector_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/James_Hector_1900.jpg/1280px-James_Hector_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/James_Hector_1900.jpg/500px-James_Hector_1900.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sir James Hector","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJames_Hector_1900.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Colonial_Museum_Wellington_1934.jpg/500px-Colonial_Museum_Wellington_1934.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Colonial_Mus